  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $16{y}^{2}-81=0.$",
  "worked_solution": "Using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{y}^{2}-81 & =0 \\\\ (4y)^{2}-(9)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (4y-9)(4y+9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n4y-9=0\\,\\text{or}\\,4y+9=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4y-9 & =0 & \\, & \\, & 4y+9 & =0 \\\\ 4y & =9 & \\, & \\, & 4y & =-9 \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{4} & \\, & \\, & y & =-\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=-\\frac{9}{4},\\frac{9}{4}.$ We can write this even more concisely as $y=\\pm \\frac{9}{4}.$",
